{"pureId": 3076712045, "externalId": "105002128981", "externalIdSource": "Scopus", "uuid": "81301a13-f828-49ab-b6c3-ad8f3c7733ef", "title": {"formatted": true, "value": "State-level disparities in cervical cancer prevention and outcomes in the United States"}, "subTitle": {"formatted": true, "value": "a modeling study"}, "peerReview": true, "numberOfPages": 10, "managingOrganisationalUnit": {"uuid": "b9513a4d-0829-4345-bd9d-ba582857d240", "link": {"ref": "content", "href": "https://experts.umn.edu/ws/api/524/organisational-units/b9513a4d-0829-4345-bd9d-ba582857d240"}, "externalId": "AUNLTGA", "externalIdSource": "synchronisedUnifiedOrganisation", "name": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Epidemiology & Community Health"}]}, "type": {"pureId": 855, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/organisation/organisationtypes/organisation/department", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Department"}]}}}, "confidential": false, "info": {"createdBy": "root", "createdDate": "2025-10-12T22:46:52.783-0500", "modifiedBy": "root", "modifiedDate": "2025-11-25T12:37:52.171-0600", "portalUrl": "https://experts.umn.edu/en/publications/81301a13-f828-49ab-b6c3-ad8f3c7733ef", "prettyURLIdentifiers": ["state-level-disparities-in-cervical-cancer-prevention-and-outcome-2"], "additionalExternalIds": [{"value": "39570647", "idSource": "PubMed"}, {"value": "105002128981", "idSource": "QABO"}, {"value": "PMC11972673", "idSource": "PubMedCentral"}, {"value": "/0000-0002-0932-9166/work/194476416", "idSource": "ORCID"}, {"value": "/0000-0001-5076-1172/work/194481034", "idSource": "ORCID"}], "previousUuids": []}, "totalScopusCitations": 1, "fieldWeightedCitationImpact": 1.2, "pages": "737-746", "volume": "117", "journalAssociation": {"pureId": 3076712046, "title": {"value": "Journal of the National Cancer Institute"}, "issn": {"value": "0027-8874"}, "journal": {"uuid": "37397060-4827-4018-9f24-a237c5fcb54d", "link": {"ref": "content", "href": "https://experts.umn.edu/ws/api/524/journals/37397060-4827-4018-9f24-a237c5fcb54d"}, "externalId": "12459", "externalIdSource": "Scopus", "externallyManaged": true, "name": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"value": "Journal of the National Cancer Institute"}]}, "type": {"pureId": 910, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/journal/journaltypes/journal/journal", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Journal"}]}}}}, "journalNumber": "4", "type": {"pureId": 4664, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/researchoutput/researchoutputtypes/contributiontojournal/article", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Article"}]}}, "category": {"pureId": 6885, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/researchoutput/category/research", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Research"}]}}, "language": {"pureId": 4214728, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/core/languages/en_US", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "English (US)"}]}}, "abstract": {"formatted": true, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "<p>Background: Despite human papillomavirus (HPV) vaccines\u2019 availability for over a decade, coverage across the United States varies. Although some states have tried to increase HPV vaccination coverage, most model-based analyses focus on national impacts. We evaluated hypothetical changes in HPV vaccination coverage at the national and state levels for California, New York, and Texas using a mathematical model. Methods: We developed a new mathematical model of HPV transmission and cervical cancer, creating national- and state-level models, incorporating country- and state-specific vaccination coverage and cervical cancer incidence and mortality. We quantified the national- and state-level impact of increasing HPV vaccination coverage to 80% by 2025 or 2030 on cervical cancer outcomes and the time to elimination defined as less than 4 per 100\u200a000 women. Results: Increasing vaccination coverage to 80% in Texas over 10 years could reduce cervical cancer incidence by 50.9% (95% credible interval [CrI] = 46.6%-56.1%) by 2100, from 1.58 (CrI = 1.19-2.09) to 0.78 (CrI = 0.57-1.02) per 100\u200a000 women. Similarly, New York could see a 27.3% (CrI = 23.9%-31.5%) reduction from 1.43 (CrI = 0.93-2.07) to 1.04 (CrI = 0.66-1.53) per 100\u200a000 women, and California a 24.4% (CrI = 20.0%-30.0%) reduction from 1.01 (CrI = 0.66-1.44) to 0.76 (CrI = 0.50-1.09) per 100\u200a000 women. Achieving 80% coverage in 5 years will provide slightly larger and sooner reductions. If the vaccination coverage levels in 2019 continue, cervical cancer elimination could occur nationally by 2051 (CrI = 2034-2064), but state timelines may vary by decades. Conclusion: Targeting an HPV vaccination coverage of 80% by 2030 will disproportionately benefit states with low coverage and higher cervical cancer incidence. Geographically focused analyses can better inform priorities.</p>"}]}, "totalNumberOfAuthors": 10, "openAccessPermission": {"pureId": 3435857, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/researchoutput/openaccesspermission/closed", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Closed"}]}}, "submissionYear": 2025, "bibliographicalNote": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Publisher Copyright:\n\u00a9 The Author(s) 2024. Published by Oxford University Press. All rights reserved."}]}, "visibility": {"key": "FREE", "value": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Public - No restriction"}]}}, "workflow": {"workflowStep": "forApproval", "value": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "For validation"}]}}, "publicationStatuses": [{"pureId": 3076712045, "externalId": "105002128981", "externalIdSource": "Scopus", "current": true, "publicationDate": {"year": 2025, "month": 4, "day": 1}, "publicationStatus": {"pureId": 798, "uri": "/dk/atira/pure/researchoutput/status/published", "term": {"formatted": false, "text": [{"locale": "en_US", "value": "Published"}]}}}], "per